✅ 6.Why is extending Thread not preferred?

Problems

1.Java supports Single Inheritance
Since Java allows a class to extend only one class:

class MyThread extends Thread ✅
class MyThread extends Thread, Employee ❌

2. Tight Coupling
The task and the thread are tightly coupled.

Task (Business Logic)
      │
Thread (Execution Mechanism)
      │
Same Class

This violates the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) because one class is responsible for both:
🔴 What to execute (business logic)
🔴 How to execute (thread management)

3. Poor Reusability
A class extending Thread is tied to that execution model.
If you later want to execute the same task using:

ExecutorService
Thread Pool
Scheduled Executor

you'll need to refactor the code.
With Runnable, the same task can be executed by any thread or executor.

4. Modern Java Uses Runnable and Executors
Modern applications rarely create threads manually.

Instead:

Business Logic
   │
   ▼
Runnable
   │
   ▼
Thread/ExecutorService/Thread Pool

This separates responsibilities:

What to execute → Runnable
How to execute   → Thread / ExecutorService

This design is more flexible, reusable, and maintainable.